  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA weak upper disturbance will cross the Ohio Valley tonight and bring\nscattered light showers to the region overnight and early Monday. Off\nof the surface, dry air is in place and these overnight showers will\nhave a very dry atmosphere to precipitate through before they will\nactually reach the ground. Any precip is expected to be generally\nlight on the order of .01-.02 inches, but a isolated spot or two \ncould see a wetting rainfall. The disparity here is noted in the\nmodels as hit and miss QPF in light rain or showers.  \n\nAhead of the short wave, a developing 20-30 knot low level jet will \nlift up into our area this evening into tonight and aid in the\ndevelopment of showers given a strong moisture transport. \n\nModels have come in a much better conformity with the next system\nthat will affect the region on Wednesday with a period of rain and\nshowers, significantly more moist than what will be seen overnight\ntonight. \n\nA cutoff low in the Upper Midwest early Wed will move ne through WI\nor possibly N through MN during the day. In either scenario, a\nnotable dry punch will be wrapped into the system and have its\nwestern edge over the IN/OH border by nightfall. Rain after nightfall\nis expected to be in the eastern reaches of the forecast area and\nmoving steadily east to be out of the picture by midnight. \n\nKey message 2) On Friday, cold advection showers will be present in \nnortherly flow behind the upper low that will hang a l/w trough over \nthe Ohio Valley and provide a source of lift to lingering llvl \nmoisture. Sfc high pressure will then build behind it and dry out the\nregion through at least Saturday. \n\nAbove normal temperatures will prevail through Friday and then drop\nto near normal which will feel notably cooler with highs in the mid\n50s and lows in the upper 30s.",
